{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791608162,"nonce":"OEOwEWV4W4zHZ6IQEE76CMj49Cw=","title":"Every process runs the static initialisers of every LLVM target","message":"llvm-sys compiles its C wrappers around llvm-c/Target.h into one object, which defines LLVM_InitializeNative* beside LLVM_InitializeAll*. inkwell's Target::initialize_native (also called inside Module::create_jit_execution_engine) references the native wrappers, so the linker loaded that object, and its All* functions then pulled in every target LLVM was configured with. inkwell's default target-all feature named each target's init functions directly as well. The llvm-backend zylua and zypy carried 642 C++ static initialisers, all LLVM's, run at every launch whether the LLVM tier is used or not: Hexagon 43, AMDGPU 28, X86 27, AArch64 25, RISCV 19, PPC 16, ARM 16, Mips 15 and the rest. The backends only ever compile for the host.\n\nFix: llvm-sys built with disable-alltargets-init, inkwell without its default target features, and the ten wrappers defined in crates/compiler/src/llvm_targets.rs for the host target (aarch64 or x86_64). initialize_host() calls them, so the object defining them is loaded whatever order a linker reads archives in; every caller (the JIT backend, zyntax_cli's AOT backend, the LLVM tests) goes through it.\n\nMeasured on the Mac (release, llvm-backend, warm page cache):\n- initialisers 642 -\u003e 401 (only AArch64's target ones remain); zylua 103.9 -\u003e 98.6 MB.\n- `zylua backend` (exits before any runtime set-up): 44.0M -\u003e 41.7M instructions, 1173 -\u003e 1089 page reclaims.\n- Wall, 30 interleaved rounds, medians: backend 7.05 -\u003e 6.93 ms, empty program 10.80 -\u003e 10.61, fib to its first statement 12.69 -\u003e 12.59 (1 to 2%).\n- ZYLUA_LLVM=1 nbody, mandelbrot, collatz and fib install LLVM code and print what lua prints.\n\nNot yet built on x86_64 Linux: the NUC was running another project's build. The definitions are referenced from the crate's own code for that reason, but the Linux link is owed before this is pushed.\n\nThe larger cost stays: LLVM in the image at all costs about 2.6 ms per process (see the companion issue).","files":null}]}